    {
      "locale": "en",
      "label": "Palindrome Pairs: match ends with a reversed trie",
      "url": "http://127.0.0.1:18770/leetcode/336/#step1",
      "excerpt": "0202 · Match outside, test insideWhat remains after matching the outside of two unequal-length words? A prefix starts at the beginning; a suffix ends at the end. Either may be empty. In s+lls, the left s matches the final s on the right, leaving ll, which must be a palindrome. If the right word is shorter, as in lls+ll, it must reverse the start of the left word. Here ll matches, and the leftover s is a palindrome too, so llsll works.s | ll | sll | s | llHow can matches share work? Insert each candidate right word j backward into a trie: a tree whose edges are characters and whose shared starting paths are stored once. The root path is empty. Path s→l→l stores reversed lls. A node’s end is the index of a right word ending exactly there, or −1. Its pal list contains indices whose unmatched original prefix is a palindrome. While inserting lls: the root leaves lls, which fails; path s leaves ll, which qualifies; path sl leaves l, which qualifies; path sll leaves the empty string, which also qualifies.This derives the invariant, a fact preserved after each move: after reading k characters of left word w, the current path equals w[:k] and the reversal of the final k characters of every reachable right word. Those outside letters need no further checks. ① If end exists, the right word is exhausted: emit only when the left suffix w[k:] is a palindrome. ② If the left word is exhausted, emit right words in that node’s pal list. A missing edge means an outside mismatch, so stop this query. Both cases exclude the same input index."
    },
    {
      "locale": "en",
      "label": "Concatenated Words: build prefixes through cuts",
      "url": "http://127.0.0.1:18770/leetcode/472/#step2",
      "excerpt": "0303 · Test each cutWhich test makes a new boundary reachable? Select a case, then test cuts while keeping its dictionary fixed. Watch the source dp[j], the piece, and destination dp[i]; step back to verify restoration. Changing the case restarts this experiment, without changing the separate prediction below.Each cell is a prefix boundary, not a character index. T/F means true/false; ? means unprocessed. A partial F is not final. Completed cells remain visible. An orange outline marks the last tested destination. The history retains every attempted cut; the search for a prefix stops at its first witness.This trace is a browser simulation, not execution of the Python submission below. Every preset has a complete trace. Teaching snapshots are separate from algorithm auxiliary space.Case (changing restarts the trace)catcat · reuse a pieceabcd · longest-prefix trapcat · exclude self-matchaaaa · multiple pathscatsdog · a gap in the middlea · one-character boundaryTarget: catcat; original dictionary: [\"cat\",\"catcat\"]← Undo one testTest next cut →Reset traceShow final stateTest position (scrub to rewind)Tests completed: 0/17; finished nonempty prefixes: 0Initial: only the empty prefix dp[0]=T; all other prefixes are unprocessed.Retained prefix statesdp[0] · empty prefixcompleteTdp[1] · cunprocessed?dp[2] · caunprocessed?dp[3] · catunprocessed?dp[4] · catcunprocessed?dp[5] · catcaunprocessed?dp[6] · catcatunprocessed?Tests so farNo cut has been tested yet.Source → target / pieceTest result"
